# Joe — git cadence (plain English)

Joe is an experienced game/product director learning software development by shipping. Prefer player/product language over terminal jargon. Never assume he knows git conventions.

## States (say which one we're in)

Use these labels exactly when reporting progress:

1. **planned** — decided, not built
2. **implemented locally** — code exists on this machine only
3. **playable locally** — can be tried in RimWorld on this machine
4. **committed** — saved as a labeled restore point in git (still only this machine / this branch)
5. **pushed** — copy is on GitHub for other machines/agents
6. **pull request opened** — waiting for review/merge
7. **approved** — review said yes
8. **merged** — on the target branch (usually `main`)
9. **deployed** — only if something beyond merge is required; otherwise stop at merged

Never say shipped / live / merged / on main unless that remote state was checked.

## Offer format (required every time)

Whenever you suggest **commit**, **push**, **pull**, **open PR**, or **merge**, use this shape:

1. **Action** — one plain-English verb (“Save a restore point”, “Send this to GitHub”, “Get the latest from GitHub”)
2. **Why now** — one concrete sentence tied to the current work (not a generic slogan)
3. **What success looks like** — one short check Joe can trust
4. **Repo facts** — repository, remote, branch, target, and whether the step is **local** or **remote**

Do not offer a git action without the **Why now** line.

## Rubric — good time to act

| Signal | Offer | Why (use the real reason, not this table text verbatim) |
|---|---|---|
| One player-facing outcome works, or one bug is fixed | **Commit** | Lock a restore point before the next change |
| Joe wants another machine, agent, or future-you to see it | **Push** | Put the restore point on GitHub so it isn’t only here |
| About to try a risky rewrite, second experiment, or large delete | **Commit first** (or stash) | Make undo cheap before the risky move |
| Diff is hard to explain in one sentence | **Stop and commit a smaller slice** | Work has drifted past a clean restore point |
| Need someone else’s merged work, or a PR just landed | **Pull** | Refresh this machine before play/review |
| Mod files / DLLs / Defs changed after pull | Say **restart RimWorld** after pull | The game only picks up those files after restart |
| Work is ready for review or merge | **Push**, then open/update PR | Review needs the remote copy |

## Too-far alert (proactive)

If any of these are true, **interrupt with a checkpoint prompt** before continuing more work:

- More than one unrelated outcome is unfinished in the same dirty tree
- The change set can no longer be described in one sentence
- A second experiment started without a commit after the first success
- Local playable work has sat uncommitted across a context switch (new chat, new agent, or “next milestone”)
- Joe is about to pull while local uncommitted work could conflict

Say it plainly, for example:

> Checkpoint: we’re past a clean restore point.  
> **Action:** Save a restore point (commit) — **local**, repo `McRimworld`, branch `<name>`.  
> **Why now:** the next edit would mix two outcomes and make undo messy.  
> **Success:** `git status` is clean and the commit message names one outcome.

## Safety (never skip)

Before any material git/GitHub action, state: **repository, remote, branch, intended target, local vs remote**.

Never:

- force-push
- rewrite history
- discard Joe’s local work
- delete a branch
- change a remote
- merge into a protected branch

…without clear authorization in that turn.

Preserve existing work. Ask only questions that change the result or prevent a safe step.

## End of substantial work

Always end with:

1. **Current state** (from the list above)
2. **One next action** (with Why now + success check)

If the next action is a git step Joe must approve or run, use the Offer format.
